* What Nix is
- declarative package manager and build system
- same input should produce the same result
- useful for reproducible dev environments and system config

* Common files
- `flake.nix` project or system entry point when using flakes
- `flake.lock` pinned inputs
- `default.nix` default expression for a package or folder
- `shell.nix` development shell without flakes
- `configuration.nix` main NixOS system config
- `home.nix` Home Manager user config

* Basic commands
@code bash
nix search nixpkgs git
nix shell nixpkgs#ripgrep
nix run nixpkgs#hello
nix develop
nix flake update
@end

* Temporary package
@code bash
nix shell nixpkgs#jq nixpkgs#fd
@end

* Dev shell with flake
@code nix
{
  description = "simple dev shell";

  inputs = {
    nixpkgs.url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-unstable";
    flake-utils.url = "github:numtide/flake-utils";
  };

  outputs = { self, nixpkgs, flake-utils }:
    flake-utils.lib.eachDefaultSystem (system:
      let
        pkgs = import nixpkgs { inherit system; };
      in {
        devShells.default = pkgs.mkShell {
          packages = with pkgs; [
            git
            nodejs
            pnpm
          ];
        };
      });
}
@end

* Enter dev shell
@code bash
nix develop
@end

* Build a flake package
@code bash
nix build .
nix build .#my-package
@end

* Run a flake app
@code bash
nix run .
nix run .#my-app
@end

* Show flake outputs
@code bash
nix flake show
@end

* Format and inspect
@code bash
nix fmt
nix repl
@end

* Nix language basics
- attribute set is `{ key = value; }`
- list is `[ a b c ]`
- string is `"text"`
- function looks like `{ pkgs }: ...` or `x: ...`
- `with pkgs; [ git jq ]` imports names from `pkgs` into scope
- `let ... in ...` defines local variables

* Useful ideas
- pin inputs with `flake.lock`
- keep dev shell minimal and task-focused
- prefer `nix develop` for project tooling
- use `nix search` when you do not know package names
- rebuild NixOS with `sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake .#hostname`

* Home Manager
@code bash
home-manager switch --flake .#user
@end

* Update one flake input
Run in the flake directory. This command uses the modern Nix CLI with flakes enabled.
@code bash
nix flake update nixpkgs
git diff -- flake.lock
@end
Inputs that follow nixpkgs may move with it. Review the lockfile and build before committing.

* Diagnose a failed build
@code bash
nix build .#my-package -L --show-trace --keep-failed
@end
`-L` prints build logs; `--show-trace` adds evaluation context. `--keep-failed` retains a failed local build directory and prints its location.
If the error reports a derivation path, inspect its recorded log:
@code bash
nix log /nix/store/replace-with-reported-path.drv
@end

* Inspect NixOS generations and roll back
These commands apply to a NixOS host, not macOS or a standalone Home Manager profile.
@code bash
sudo nix-env --profile /nix/var/nix/profiles/system --list-generations
sudo nixos-rebuild switch --rollback
@end
Rollback activates the previous system generation. It does not revert configuration files or application data; fix the flake before the next rebuild.

* Inspect Home Manager generations
@code bash
home-manager generations
@end
To reactivate a retained generation, run its `activate` script using the store path shown above:
@code bash
/nix/store/replace-with-generation-home-manager-generation/activate
@end

References: {https://nix.dev/manual/nix/stable/command-ref/new-cli/nix3-flake-update.html}[flake update], {https://nix.dev/manual/nix/stable/command-ref/new-cli/nix3-build.html}[build], {https://nixos.org/manual/nixos/stable/#sec-rollback}[NixOS rollback], {https://nix-community.github.io/home-manager/index.xhtml#sec-usage-rollbacks}[Home Manager rollback].
